<BaseLayout title="Product Workshop — Matthew Cochran" description="A disciplined product-development workshop for turning ambiguous opportunities into executable, verifiable product systems.">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Product Workshop / Method" title="From consequential idea to dependable product." description="A practical operating system for discovery, risk analysis, planning, construction, and verification—designed for teams that need decisions and working systems, not workshop theater." aside="Discovery → Risk → Plan → Build → Verify" />
  <section class="section shell workshop-intro"><div><p class="kicker">Working premise</p><h2>Ambiguity should decrease at every stage.</h2></div><div><p>The workshop makes product, project, and technical assumptions visible before they become expensive. Each phase produces durable artifacts that constrain the next: evidence, boundaries, dependency models, executable plans, and verification records.</p></div></section>
  <section class="workshop-phases shell">
    <article><span>01 / Discover</span><h2>Establish the problem worth solving.</h2><p>Elicit customer context, jobs, constraints, willingness, and disconfirming evidence. Separate market facts from internal enthusiasm.</p><ul><li>ICP and stakeholder map</li><li>Jobs and outcomes</li><li>Evidence ledger</li><li>Go / change / stop decision</li></ul></article>
    <article><span>02 / Model risk</span><h2>Find failure before failure finds the product.</h2><p>Use FMECA, boundary analysis, threat modeling, and operational scenarios to identify what can break and what must be controlled.</p><ul><li>Failure modes and criticality</li><li>Trust boundaries</li><li>Safety and privacy constraints</li><li>Required evidence</li></ul></article>
    <article><span>03 / Plan</span><h2>Turn intent into an executable path.</h2><p>Model dependencies and critical paths, define decision rights, and sequence work around risk retirement rather than activity volume.</p><ul><li>CPM network and milestones</li><li>Risk-first increments</li><li>Acceptance criteria</li><li>Human and agent responsibilities</li></ul></article>
    <article><span>04 / Build</span><h2>Keep authority in the system.</h2><p>Implement with explicit contracts, modular boundaries, policy gates, observability, and reversible delivery practices.</p><ul><li>Architecture and interfaces</li><li>Incremental releases</li><li>Automated checks</li><li>Operational runbooks</li></ul></article>
    <article><span>05 / Verify</span><h2>Earn confidence with evidence.</h2><p>Test the claims that matter, record what was observed, and make release decisions against defined thresholds.</p><ul><li>Product and technical validation</li><li>Security and resilience evidence</li><li>Release readiness</li><li>Learning loop</li></ul></article>
  </section>
  <section class="methodology"><div class="method-grid"></div><div class="shell method-content"><p class="kicker">Applied tooling</p><h2>The method is becoming software.</h2><p>Praxec cognitive architectures and deterministic MCP tools—including CPM planning, elicitation, FMECA, cross-matrix analysis, and scientific process—bring the same discipline into agentic engineering workflows.</p><a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/praxec" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore Praxec ↗</a></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
